\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Measuring $L_{\text  {adapt}}$: four routing strategies on the same evolved tree.} Each dot shows end-of-stream performance (pass rate or CWR) with bootstrap 95\% CI, across all three benchmarks. \emph  {Oracle}: per-task best branch (upper bound on adaptation). \emph  {Adapt}: our navigator routes per task. \emph  {Naive}: always use \texttt  {main} branch. \emph  {Worst}: per-task worst branch (lower bound). All four share identical evolver effort; only the routing decision differs. The gap between Oracle and Naive upper-bounds $L_{\text  {adapt}}$; Adapt recovers most of it (CTF-Dojo: 35\% vs.\ Oracle 55\%; PolyBench: $+$6\% CWR vs.\ Oracle $+$12\%), confirming that per-task routing substantially reduces the adaptation loss.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq3_stats}{{4}{7}{\textbf {Measuring $\Ladapt $: four routing strategies on the same evolved tree.} Each dot shows end-of-stream performance (pass rate or CWR) with bootstrap 95\% CI, across all three benchmarks. \emph {Oracle}: per-task best branch (upper bound on adaptation). \emph {Adapt}: our navigator routes per task. \emph {Naive}: always use \texttt {main} branch. \emph {Worst}: per-task worst branch (lower bound). All four share identical evolver effort; only the routing decision differs. The gap between Oracle and Naive upper-bounds $\Ladapt $; Adapt recovers most of it (CTF-Dojo: 35\% vs.\ Oracle 55\%; PolyBench: $+$6\% CWR vs.\ Oracle $+$12\%), confirming that per-task routing substantially reduces the adaptation loss.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
